  "query_preview": "..(Wednesday through Monday)\nIssued at 320 PM EST MON NOV 2 2026\n\nTuesday Afternoon Through Sunday\n\nSomewhat active weather is progged to return during this period \nstarting Wednesday. A large and mature upper level trough will \ncontinue towards the region at the start of the period bringing a \nsurface low and a trailing cold front. At the time of our rain \nchances the surface low will be near The Great Lakes and the cold \nfront will drape all the way south to the Gulf of America. Our rain \nchances Wednesday will come from that cold front as it moves east. \nWith plenty of moisture and the front providing a forcing mechanism, \nat least scattered showers are expected throughout the day on \nWednesday. A few hundred j/kg of MUCAPE could support some thunder \nand heavier rain at times, but chances for organized severe weather \nseem to be nill. Rain chances should exit eastern KY sometime \nWednesday night leading to dry conditions on Thursday and highs in \nthe low-mid 70s. \n\nShortly thereafter another upper level wave ejects and quickly moves \nthrough the region to end the work week. Model guidance don't really \nshow any PoPs associated with this wave despite a 500mb vort max and \nnotable 500mb height falls. A look at forecast soundings show a \npretty dry column, probably from moisture being wiped out from \nWednesday's cold front and the atmosphere not being able to recover \nyet. Dry air certainly makes sense, but am wondering if models are \noverdoing the amount of dry air. Either way it'll need to be watched \nto see if we start to trend wetter. Currently thinking most of the \narea will stay dry Thursday and Friday but a few isolated showers \ncan't be ruled out. Look for highs on Friday to be around 70. \n\nScattered rain chances will likely remain for the weekend as models \nsuggest one or two more quick trough ejections move through the \nregion. Confidence is lower on the evolution of rain chances this \nweekend, but given the upper level pattern, rain chances seem \nprobable. Highs will be more mild in the upper 50s to lower 60s. \nSomething worth noting in terms of temperatures is that by this \nweekend and into next week we will have a negative AO, NAO, and \npositive PNA (cold phases). This lends towards an increased \npotential for us to see moments of below average temperatures into \nat least early next week.",
